{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Los Angeles Angels entered the season carrying the familiar weight of expectations — a two-way unicorn in Shohei Ohtani playing out the final year of his contract, Mike Trout flashing brilliance when healthy, and a supporting cast that seemed perpetually one piece short. Through April and May, the Angels played surprisingly competitive baseball, hovering around .500 and showing enough fight to keep hope alive. Early bullpen misfires and untimely homers surrendered were offset by clutch hits from Hunter Renfroe, Luis Rengifo, and Trout himself, giving the Halos a puncher's chance on most nights.\n\nJune and early July maintained that uneasy equilibrium. The rotation was inconsistent, the bullpen leaked runs at the worst moments — Chris Devenski surrendering a grand slam to Elias Diaz being a particularly painful snapshot — but the offense kept answering. Mike Moustakas delivered a three-run shot, Ohtani launched his 35th home run, and backup Michael Stefanic provided walk-off magic, keeping the Angels at or near .500 deep into the summer. For a team that would finish well under .500, the first four months were a genuine competitive effort.\n\nThen August arrived, and the season collapsed with brutal swiftness. The Angels went 8-19, their worst month by a wide margin, and the high-leverage plays told a grim story: Carlos Estévez — brilliant in relief all year — was suddenly surrendering grand slams and walk-off doubles, while the rotation offered little shelter. A Bryce Harper two-run blast off Matt Moore on August 30th epitomized the cruelty of the month, though even that ugly night had a counterpunch with Brandon Drury's home run. The damage was done. The playoff window had slammed shut.\n\nSeptember brought the melancholy clarity of a lost season. With Ohtani's Angels future unresolved and the roster increasingly a showcase for young players like Kyren Paris, Logan O'Hoppe, and Nolan Schanuel, the Angels went 8-19 again but played with a looser, younger energy. Brett Phillips hit a solo homer that meant nothing in the standings and everything to his own story. Brandon Drury kept mashing. The season ended at 73-89, a quiet goodbye to Ohtani's extraordinary Angel tenure, and a franchise left to reckon with what comes next.",
  "chapters": [
